\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Relabeling transports the state and its marginal}
\date{2026-10-17}

\p{Let #{M,M'} be finite-dimensional real positive inner-product
spaces with their [induced Borel Euclidean volumes](fdip-002H)
#{\lambda,\lambda'}.
Let #{Q:M\to M} be positive and self-adjoint, and let
#{P:M\to M'} be a linear isometry equivalence. Put #{Q'=PQP^{-1}}.
Then #{Q'} is positive and self-adjoint, and the
[Gaussian laws](fdip-0005) satisfy
##{Z_{Q'}=Z_Q,\qquad P_*\mu_Q=\mu_{Q'}.}
Borel pushforward means #{(P_*\mu)(A)=\mu(P^{-1}(A))} for every
Borel set #{A}, extending the [finite convention](ftip-007S).
For every real or complex Borel function #{F'},
##{\begin{aligned}
\int|F'|\,d\mu_{Q'}&=\int|F'\circ P|\,d\mu_Q,\\
F'\in L^1(\mu_{Q'})&\Longleftrightarrow F'\circ P\in L^1(\mu_Q).
\end{aligned}}
The first identity allows infinity. Under the equivalent finite
absolute-integral conditions,
##{\omega_{Q'}(F')=\omega_Q(F'\circ P).}
In particular, the target observable transported from #{F} is
#{F'=F\circ P^{-1}}. Exact and signed arrows of
[real backgrounds](fdip-000G) supply such isometries.}

\proof{
\p{The [adjoint identity](fdip-003D) gives #{P^\dagger=P^{-1}},
so #{Q'} is self-adjoint. For #{z'=Px\ne0},
#{b_{M'}(z',Q'z')=b_M(x,Qx)>0}; this also proves equality of
Gaussian actions at #{Px} and #{x}. The
[isometry-volume theorem](fdip-002L) gives #{P_*\lambda=\lambda'}.
Consequently, for every nonnegative Borel #{h'},
##{\begin{aligned}
&\int h'(z')e^{-\mathcal A_{Q'}(z')}\,d\lambda'(z')\\
&\qquad=\int h'(Px)e^{-\mathcal A_Q(x)}\,d\lambda(x).
\end{aligned}}
Take #{h'=1}; the [positive Gaussian integral](fdip-002T) gives
finite nonzero normalizers, and this equality proves #{Z_{Q'}=Z_Q}.
Then take #{h'=\mathbf1_A/Z_Q} to obtain
#{\mu_{Q'}(A)=\mu_Q(P^{-1}(A))} for every Borel #{A}.}

\p{Taking #{h'=|F'|/Z_Q} gives the extended absolute-integral
identity and hence the integrability equivalence. If finite,
positive and negative parts give the expectation identity; real and
imaginary parts give the complex version. Every real polynomial
function is integrable by [absolute Gaussian moments](fdip-002W).
On that algebra the expectation identity agrees with the previously
proved [functional transport](fdip-003F). No algebra structure on all
of #{L^1} is required.}

\p{For linear functionals #{\ell',r'\in(M')^\vee}, finite second
moments and the pushforward give the intrinsic covariance identity
##{\operatorname{Cov}_{Q'}(\ell',r')
 =\operatorname{Cov}_{Q}(\ell'\circ P,r'\circ P).}
The [inverse-precision covariance theorem](fdip-000B) says that,
relative to the specified pairings,
#{\operatorname{Cov}_Q(b_M(u,-),b_M(v,-))=b_M(u,Q^{-1}v)}.
Since #{b_{M'}(u',Px)=b_M(P^{-1}u',x)}, this yields
##{G'=PGP^{-1},\qquad G=Q^{-1}.}
In orthonormal coordinates this is #{G'=PGP^T}; an arbitrary linear
coordinate map gives covariance congruence instead, as in
\ref{fdip-003M}. Equality of second moments was not used to infer the
pushforward law.}
}

\p{Now let #{M=X\oplus Y} and #{M'=X'\oplus Y'} be orthogonal
splittings that #{P} carries by isometries #{P_X,P_Y}, so the
[split-square hypotheses](fdip-003E) hold. For the four components,
##{\begin{aligned}
A'&=P_XAP_X^{-1},&B'&=P_XBP_Y^{-1},\\
E'&=P_YEP_X^{-1},&C'&=P_YCP_Y^{-1}.
\end{aligned}}
Here #{E=B^\dagger}, and #{C>0}. The retained precisions and
eliminated Gaussian factors satisfy
##{\begin{aligned}
S'&=P_XSP_X^{-1},& S&=A-BC^{-1}E>0,\\
c_{C'}&=c_C,&c_C&=\frac{(2\pi)^{\dim Y/2}}{\sqrt{\det C}},\\
Z_{S'}&=Z_S,&(P_X)_*\mu_S&=\mu_{S'}.
\end{aligned}}
Thus the complete fiber scalar and the additive effective-action
constant #{-\log c_C} are preserved, along with the normalized law.}

\proof{
\p{The four blocks and Schur conjugacy are \ref{fdip-003E}.
A basis of #{Y} and its image under #{P_Y} give the same array
for #{C} and #{C'}, hence equal determinants; the dimensions are
also equal. This proves #{c_{C'}=c_C}. The first part of the theorem
applied on #{X} proves #{Z_{S'}=Z_S}.
The [exact integration formula](fdip-000D), for the induced product
volumes, is
##{\int_Y e^{-\mathcal A_Q(x,y)}\,d\lambda_Y(y)
 =c_Ce^{-b_X(x,Sx)/2}.}
The [event marginal theorem](fdip-000E) proves
#{\pi_*\mu_Q=\mu_S} and #{\pi'_*\mu_{Q'}=\mu_{S'}}.
Pushforwards compose by inverse images of Borel sets. Using
#{\pi'P=P_X\pi}, one obtains
##{\begin{aligned}
(P_X)_*\mu_S
 &=(P_X\pi)_*\mu_Q\\
 &=(\pi'P)_*\mu_Q=\pi'_*\mu_{Q'}=\mu_{S'}.
\end{aligned}}
This proves moving marginal transport from actual measures, rather
than from Schur or covariance identities alone. The same marginal
supplier gives the extended absolute-integral identity for retained
observables and the polynomial-state restriction.}
}

\p{For vertex bijections #{p:V\to V'}, take a nonempty proper
#{R\subsetneq V}, set #{R'=pR}, and use the coordinate splits.
The map #{P_R:\mathbb R^R\to\mathbb R^{R'}} has the genuine moving
target #{\mathbb R^{pR}}. At #{p(v)}, #{v\in R}, both
#{\pi_{R'}Pf} and #{P_R\pi_Rf} have value #{f(v)}. Thus:}

\tikzfigs{0.75}{\begin{tikzpicture}[
  every node/.style={inner sep=4pt},
  transport/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]},shorten >=2pt,shorten <=2pt}
]
\node (v) at (0,0) {$\mathbb R^V$};
\node (vp) at (3.4,0) {$\mathbb R^{V'}$};
\node (r) at (0,-2) {$\mathbb R^R$};
\node (rp) at (3.4,-2) {$\mathbb R^{R'}$};
\draw[transport] (v) -- node[above=2mm] {$P$} (vp);
\draw[transport] (r) -- node[above=2mm] {$P_R$} (rp);
\draw[transport] (v) -- node[left=2mm] {$\pi_R$} (r);
\draw[transport] (vp) -- node[right=2mm] {$\pi_{R'}$} (rp);
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{The lower map exists because the retained subset is transported.
Keeping the target labels fixed can select an inequivalent marginal;
the [adjacent-versus-opposite example](fdip-003J) gives both the
precision calculation and a failure of projection factorization.}

\p{These conclusions use positive precisions and the stated product
volumes. Fixing an eliminated field, taking a
[conditional-density kernel](fdip-003A), and taking a marginal are
different operations. A singular full-fiber integral is not repaired
by a pseudoinverse: [changing the integration space](fdip-0039)
changes the measure. The [massless projected cycle law](fdip-000F)
has its proved density on the specified retained image, and is not
obtained by inserting a mean-zero constraint into the unconstrained
marginal formula.}

\p{\citet{Proposition B.55, p. 505}{friedli2017statistical}
gives the Gaussian change-of-variables convention.}

\p{\citet{Theorem 1.7.15, pp. 200-202}{tao2011measure}
gives the nonnegative integration convention on the Borel product
measures; absolute integrability precedes signed subtraction.}

\p{\citet{Appendix A.5.5, pp. 650-651}{boyd2004convex}
gives the real block convention, with its
first pivot exchanged to #{C}.}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{LinearIsometryEquiv.measurePreserving}
supplies volume preservation for finite-dimensional real linear
isometry equivalences. TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.Probability.map_affine_multivariateGaussian}
supplies covariance-parameter transport. Set mean and shift to zero,
linear map to #{P}, and covariance to #{Q^{-1}}; the
[precision-density identification](fdip-000B) and the proved inverse
identity align it with this law. These interfaces do not supply the
endpoint data, declared partition or its counterexample.}
